2026-10-28T17:40:04.8443114Z {"timestamp":"2026-10-28T17:39:12.684Z","event":"rpc_response","_schema":"rpc-message/v2","direction":"IN","server_id":"safeoutputs","payload":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{"ttlMs":0,"cacheScope":"","tools":[{"description":"WRITE-ONCE: do NOT probe this tool with empty or placeholder arguments. If not ready to post a real comment, call noop. Adds a comment to an existing GitHub issue, pull request, or discussion; use create_issue, create_discussion, or create_pull_request for new items. IMPORTANT CONSTRAINTS: Input is limited to 524288 characters, 10 mentions, and configured max-links URL occurrences (default: 50). Oversized agent content is automatically replaced by notices to fit the complete 65536-character published comment. Markers and footers are preserved in full; publication fails if protected content alone cannot fit. Generated footer links do not count toward max-links. Discussion comments require safe-outputs.add-comment.discussions: true. CONSTRAINTS: Maximum 1 comment(s) can be added. Maximum 5 link(s) in the comment body, excluding generated metadata. Target: 27. NOTE: Discussion comments are disabled for this workflow because discussions:write permission is not available. Set 'discussions: true' in the workflow's safe-outputs.add-comment configuration to enable discussion comments and request this permission. This tool records a real comment intent. Do not use it for placeholder comments, auth checks, or probing. Call it only when the final comment body is ready; otherwise use noop or report_incomplete.","inputSchema":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"body":{"description":"Final intended Markdown comment, not placeholder text. Use body, not comment_body. Maximum input: 524288 characters, 10 mentions, and configured max-links URL occurrences including repeats (default: 50). Large agent-owned text or structured-data nodes may be replaced with notices to fit 65536 published characters including attribution. Markers and footers are never shortened or omitted; protected-content overflow fails explicitly.","maxLength":524288,"type":"string"},"comment_id":{"description":"Existing issue or pull request comment ID to update. Only valid when the workflow config sets safe-outputs.add-comment.target to '*' and the ID is present in safe-outputs.add-comment.allows-comment-ids. Use this only for comment IDs precomputed by trusted workflow steps.","type":["number","string"]},"integrity":{"description":"Trustworthiness level of the message source (e.g., \"low\", \"medium\", \"high\").","type":"string"},"item_number":{"description":"The issue, pull request, or discussion number to comment on. This is the numeric ID from the GitHub URL (e.g., 123 in github.com/owner/repo/issues/123). Can also be a temporary_id from a previously created issue in the same workflow run — use the '#aw_abc123' form; the bare 'aw_abc123' form is also accepted and normalised to '#aw_abc123'. If omitted, the tool auto-targets the issue, PR, or discussion that triggered this workflow. Auto-targeting only works for issue, pull_request, discussion, and comment event triggers — it does NOT work for schedule, workflow_dispatch, push, or workflow_run triggers. For those trigger types, always provide item_number explicitly, or the tool call will fail with an error. Required when safe-outputs.add-comment.target is '*' (any item): calls without item_number (or pr_number/pr alias) are rejected. NOTE: this field is named item_number, NOT issue_number.","type":["number","string"]},"pr":{"description":"Alias for item_number when targeting a pull request. Prefer item_number in new calls.","type":["number","string"]},"pr_number":{"description":"Alias for item_number when targeting a pull request. Prefer item_number in new calls.","type":["number","string"]},"reply_to_id":{"description":"Node ID of the discussion comment to reply to, enabling threaded discussion comments. When provided, the new comment is posted as a reply to t